I haven't played that "S" game, but comments here
suggest it is much more difficult to survive the
early levels: "NetHack doesn't care if you live or
die, but SlashEm wants you DEAD" is one approximate
comment paraphrase. Still, to someone who hasn't
practiced the tactics, the protection racket looks
"impossible" even in vanilla, so maybe it is a
matter of more dance practice still needed.

There's something way counterintuitive about running
away from a monster in a closed path provided by
room connectivity, hoping your stupid pet shows up
before you've done enough circuits to starve to
death, but the tactic is successful more times than
not, and with clever dodging, you can end up with
multiple pursuers, a bit of a giggle.

It helps, in this regard, to locate and unlock every
single door on a level, so your pet doesn't so often
get trapped away from where you need it to be.

It helps, in general, to be able to realize the
times when the protection racket is just going to
get you killed, and unleash a flurry of daggers or a
quick wand zap, force bolt, or whatever, and have
available other ways to play the game as well, when
and if you must abandon the protection racket.

It helps, specifically, to realize that the
protection racket is still successful if you arrive
in front of the priest with XP 0 or XP 19, so you
_can_ snuff a very few particularly annoying
monsters along the way, just try to delay that to
the really needful ones.

The protection racket is quite soul satisfying When
It Works, but the RNG is not a friend to protection
racketeers [or anyone else trying to survive
NetHack, or presumably, SlashEm; is it true your RNG
has _both_ The Jaws That Bite _and_ The Claws That
Snatch?] so play prudently.

